window.__hvBdRegister("laws", {"key":"laws","sections":[{"i":0,"j":50,"loc":"624a–628e","title":"Divine lawgivers and the aim of war","summary":"On the walk from Cnosus to the cave of Zeus, the Athenian Stranger asks Cleinias of Crete and Megillus of Sparta about the origin of their laws, which they ascribe to Zeus and Apollo. Cleinias explains that common meals, gymnastics and light arms were all designed for war, since every city is by nature always at war with every other. The Athenian extends this to villages, households and the individual, who may be superior or inferior to himself. Using the example of quarrelling brothers, he argues that the best judge reconciles the parties rather than destroying one side, so the lawgiver should aim at peace and goodwill, ordering war for the sake of peace, not peace for the sake of war.","ideas":["Cleinias holds that Cretan institutions all look to war","Victory over oneself is the first and best victory","The best judge reconciles a divided family with laws","Civil war is worse than foreign war; peace, not war, is best"],"terms":[{"t":"syssitia (common meals)","d":"The public messes of Crete and Sparta, which Cleinias says were instituted with a view to war."},{"t":"stasis (civil strife)","d":"Internal war within a city, which every lawgiver would most wish to prevent or end."}]},{"i":51,"j":66,"loc":"628e–632e","title":"Tyrtaeus, Theognis and the whole of virtue","summary":"The Athenian sets the Spartan poet Tyrtaeus, who praised only courage in foreign war, against Theognis, who praised loyalty in civil strife. Loyalty in civil war requires all of virtue, whereas many mercenaries are brave yet unjust and senseless, so courage ranks only fourth. He insists that Minos and Lycurgus legislated for the whole of virtue, not its lowest part. He then sketches how Cleinias should have described the Cretan laws: human goods (health, beauty, strength, wealth) depend on divine goods (wisdom, temperance, justice, courage), and the laws should guide citizens through marriage, education, passions, property and death, under guardians led by mind. They agree to examine the virtues one by one, starting with courage.","ideas":["Courage alone is the fourth and lowest part of virtue","Human goods depend on the divine goods of the soul","Laws should be classed by the virtues, not by occasional needs","Mind binds all ordinances in harmony with temperance and justice"],"terms":[{"t":"aretē (virtue)","d":"Excellence as a whole, of which wisdom, temperance, justice and courage are the ranked parts."},{"t":"divine and human goods","d":"The Athenian's ranking of virtues of the soul above bodily goods and wealth, the latter depending on the former."}]},{"i":67,"j":98,"loc":"632e–636a","title":"Courage against pleasure; temperance questioned","summary":"Megillus lists Spartan institutions aimed at courage: common meals, gymnastics, hunting, and endurance of pain such as the Crypteia. The Athenian asks whether courage must also resist pleasures and flattery, and the Dorians admit their laws train citizens against pain but not against pleasure. Noting that old men may criticize the laws in private, he argues that those never exposed to great pleasures will be enslaved by them, half slave and half free. Turning to temperance, he observes that gymnasia and common meals can foster faction and unnatural sexual practices, and he concludes that legislation turns largely on pleasure and pain, the two fountains nature lets flow.","ideas":["True courage withstands pleasure as well as pain","Cretan and Spartan laws train endurance of pain only","Only elders should discuss defects of the laws, and privately","Happiness depends on drawing rightly from pleasure and pain"],"terms":[{"t":"Crypteia","d":"A Spartan practice of roaming the country by day and night without shoes or beds, cited by Megillus as training in endurance."},{"t":"sōphrosynē (temperance)","d":"Self-control in pleasures, the virtue the Athenian finds poorly provided for in Dorian institutions."}]},{"i":99,"j":141,"loc":"636e–640e","title":"Drinking parties and the need for a sober leader","summary":"Megillus defends the Spartan ban on revelry and drunkenness; the Athenian replies that every people defends its own customs, and that victory in war proves little about the goodness of institutions. He criticizes praising or blaming practices without examining their proper use, comparing it to condemning goats seen without a goatherd. Since a drinking party is a kind of gathering that needs a ruler, it can only be fairly judged when well conducted, which he says he has never seen. The leader of such a party, like a general, must be sober and wise, able to preserve and increase friendship. Cleinias asks what great good a well-run feast could bring.","ideas":["Victories and defeats are doubtful tests of laws","A practice must be judged in its right use, not its abuse","Every gathering, including a drinking party, needs a ruler","The master of the feast must be sober and wise"],"terms":[{"t":"symposion (drinking party)","d":"A convivial gathering for wine, which the Athenian proposes to evaluate when properly ordered."},{"t":"methē (intoxication)","d":"Drunkenness, the subject the Athenian distinguishes from simply drinking or not drinking wine."}]},{"i":142,"j":161,"loc":"641a–644c","title":"Education defined and the puppet image","summary":"The Athenian answers that well-ordered feasts belong to education, and education makes good men who conquer in battle, though victory can breed insolence. After apologizing for Athenian talkativeness, he is welcomed by Megillus, whose family are Athens' proxeni, and Cleinias, a kinsman of Epimenides. He defines education as training from childhood, in play, toward the excellence of citizenship, knowing how to rule and be ruled; training for wealth or mere cleverness does not deserve the name. He then describes each person as a puppet of the gods pulled by the cords of pleasure, pain, fear and hope, and urges that one follow the soft golden cord of reasoning, which the city calls law.","ideas":["Education is training in virtue from childhood, through play","Real education aims at ruling and being ruled justly","Humans are puppets pulled by opposing cords of feeling","The golden cord of reason, as public law, should be followed"],"terms":[{"t":"paideia (education)","d":"Training in virtue from youth that makes a person desire to be a perfect citizen."},{"t":"golden cord","d":"The gentle pull of reasoning, embodied in the city's common law, set against the iron cords of the passions."},{"t":"proxenos","d":"A citizen who represents and hosts the interests of another city; Megillus' family serves Athens this way."}]},{"i":162,"j":242,"loc":"644c–649d","title":"Wine as a test of character","summary":"The Athenian notes that wine intensifies pleasures and passions while weakening memory and judgment, making a man a child again. Like medicine or gymnastic training, a voluntary temporary harm may bring later benefit. He distinguishes fear of evil from fear of disgrace, which the lawgiver honours as reverence or shame. Imagining a fear-potion as a test of courage, he argues that wine works as the opposite, a test of boldness and shamelessness, which must be trained against just as courage is trained among fears. A drinking party is thus a safe, cheap and quick touchstone of character, useful to the political art that cares for souls.","ideas":["Drink returns a man to the condition of a child","Shame before others is a fear the lawgiver should cultivate","Wine tests and trains resistance to shamelessness","Knowing souls is useful to the art of politics"],"terms":[{"t":"aidōs (reverence, shame)","d":"The fear of disgrace that the Athenian calls a divine fear and the opposite of insolence."},{"t":"fear-potion","d":"An imagined drug inducing terror, used to show how wine might serve as a test of character."}]},{"i":243,"j":287,"loc":"650b–656d","title":"Education through choruses and fixed music","summary":"Opening Book II, the Athenian defines education as the right training of a child's pleasures and pains, so that one loves and hates what one should before reason arrives. The gods gave festivals, the Muses, Apollo and Dionysus, and a sense of rhythm and harmony, so education begins in the chorus of song and dance. The well educated person delights in good song and dance and is offended by bad; postures and melodies expressing virtue are good. Since people take pleasure in what suits their character, enjoying bad music makes one like it, so poets must not compose as they please. He praises Egypt, where sacred forms of music and art were fixed and have not changed for ages.","ideas":["Education trains pleasure and pain before reason develops","Choral song and dance are the first education","Delight in vicious music assimilates the soul to vice","Egypt fixed its sacred music and art by law"],"terms":[{"t":"choreia (choral art)","d":"Song and dance together, given by the gods and treated as the core of early education."},{"t":"rhythm and harmony","d":"Order in bodily motion and in the voice, which humans alone among animals perceive."}]},{"i":288,"j":317,"loc":"656d–659d","title":"Who should judge music by pleasure","summary":"The Athenian imagines a festival prize for whoever gives most pleasure: small children would choose puppets, older boys comedy, women and young men tragedy, old men epic recitation. He grants that music should be judged by pleasure, but by the pleasure of the best and best educated, ideally of one man pre-eminent in virtue. The judge must teach the audience rather than learn from it, resisting the crowd's clamour; judging by show of hands has corrupted poets and theatres alike. He concludes that education draws children toward the right reason the law declares and the eldest approve, using songs as charms and compelling poets to portray good men.","ideas":["Each age prefers a different kind of entertainment","The best music pleases the best-educated judge","Judges must instruct the audience, not flatter it","Songs are charms that tune the young to the law"],"terms":[{"t":"epōdai (charms, chants)","d":"Songs designed to enchant young souls into feeling joy and sorrow in harmony with the law."},{"t":"kritēs (judge)","d":"The festival judge, who must have wisdom and courage to resist the spectators' taste."}]},{"i":318,"j":343,"loc":"660a–663e","title":"The just life is the pleasantest","summary":"Cleinias notes that only Crete and Sparta regulate music; the Athenian clarifies he describes what should be, not what is. He says the poets must teach that the just man is happy whether rich or poor, and that all so-called goods are evils to the unjust. Cleinias resists the claim that a powerful unjust man lives unpleasantly, and the Athenian argues that neither gods nor fathers could consistently teach that the justest life is not the pleasantest. He adds that the better soul's judgment is truer, and even if this were false it would be the most useful lie for a lawgiver; the myth of Cadmus shows how readily the young believe.","ideas":["The so-called goods are good only for the just","A lawgiver cannot separate the just life from the happy one","The good soul's judgment of pleasure is the truer one","A whole city should repeat one beneficial belief in song"],"terms":[{"t":"eudaimonia (happiness)","d":"The good life, which the Athenian holds belongs to the just regardless of fortune."},{"t":"useful lie","d":"The Athenian's point that even if untrue, teaching that justice is pleasantest would benefit the young."}]},{"i":344,"j":381,"loc":"663e–667a","title":"Three choruses and the Dionysian elders","summary":"The Athenian proposes three choruses to sing that the best life is the happiest: children, young men under thirty, and men from thirty to sixty, with the oldest telling stories. Cleinias is puzzled by a chorus of older men for Dionysus. Because older men are ashamed to sing, the Athenian proposes laws on wine: none before eighteen, moderation until thirty, and after forty the elders may invoke Dionysus, whose wine softens the soul like iron in fire. Cleinias says Cretans and Spartans know only camp songs; the Athenian says their herd-like training of youth neglects the individual education that makes rulers, and Cleinias again protests.","ideas":["All citizens should continually sing the same noble beliefs","Wine helps older men overcome shame and sing","Graded laws limit drinking by age","Dorian camp-style training neglects individual education"],"terms":[{"t":"Dionysian chorus","d":"The proposed chorus of older citizens, loosened by wine, who choose and sing the best songs."},{"t":"Paean","d":"A name of Apollo invoked by the young men's chorus."}]},{"i":382,"j":419,"loc":"667a–670b","title":"Correctness in imitation and the competent judge","summary":"The Athenian distinguishes the charm or pleasure accompanying a thing from its rightness or utility, such as healthfulness in food or truth in learning. Pleasure is the right standard only for harmless amusement; imitative arts, including all music, must be judged by truth, that is by faithful rendering of quantity and quality. A competent judge must know what is imitated, whether it is rendered truly, and whether it is well executed. Human poets confuse styles and separate music from words, producing meaningless display. The elder choristers must therefore learn rhythm and harmony well enough to choose songs suited to them and to lead the young toward virtue.","ideas":["Pleasure accompanies things but does not define their correctness","Music is imitation and must be judged by truth","A judge must know the original, the truth and the execution","Instrumental music without words obscures meaning"],"terms":[{"t":"mimēsis (imitation)","d":"Representation, which the Athenian says all music is, and which must be judged by its likeness to its object."},{"t":"orthotēs (correctness)","d":"The rightness of an imitation, as distinct from the pleasure it gives."}]},{"i":420,"j":455,"loc":"670b–673c","title":"Laws of the symposium and limits on wine","summary":"The Athenian returns to drinking: wine makes drinkers bold and malleable, so laws of the banquet should instil reverence, enforced by sober leaders over sixty whose authority is like a general's. Well-regulated feasts would send companions away better friends. He rejects the story that Dionysus gave wine out of revenge, holding it was given to instil modesty and health. With music completed as half of the choral art, he notes that gymnastic, the training of bodily movement, remains. He closes the topic by approving drinking only as disciplined training in temperance, and favouring Carthaginian-style bans for soldiers, slaves, magistrates, pilots, judges and others.","ideas":["Banquet laws should turn boldness into reverence","Sober elders must command the drinking party","Wine was given as a remedy, not a curse","Wine should be forbidden in many public and private roles"],"terms":[{"t":"gymnastikē (gymnastic)","d":"The training of bodily movement for the body's excellence, the other half of the choral art."},{"t":"commander of the feast","d":"The sober leader of a drinking party, over sixty, whose orders must be obeyed like a general's."}]},{"i":456,"j":524,"loc":"673c–679e","title":"After the flood: the first survivors","summary":"Opening Book III, the Athenian proposes to study the origin of government by viewing the vast stretch of time in which countless cities have risen and fallen. Ancient traditions tell of floods and plagues that left only remnants; after the great flood, only hill shepherds survived, without arts, metals or the vices of cities. Cleinias notes how recent the famous inventions are. The Athenian describes the survivors as few, glad of one another's company, with ample pasture and simple crafts, neither poor nor rich. Lacking envy and guile and believing what they were told about gods and men, they were simpler, braver, more temperate and more just than later people.","ideas":["Constitutions are to be studied over vast spans of time","Repeated catastrophes destroy cities and their arts","The survivors lacked both wealth and poverty","Simplicity made the early people good and just"],"terms":[{"t":"kataklysmos (deluge)","d":"The great flood after which only mountain shepherds remained."},{"t":"Epimenides","d":"The Cretan sage whom the Athenian calls an inventor of yesterday."}]},{"i":525,"j":569,"loc":"679e–682e","title":"From patriarchy to Troy and the Dorians","summary":"The Athenian says the first people lived by custom, without letters or lawgivers, under the rule of the eldest, as Homer describes the Cyclopes. Families joined into larger settlements at the foot of the mountains, each bringing its own customs; chosen arbiters then selected among them, becoming the first legislators and forming aristocracy or monarchy. A third stage came when people settled the plain, as at Ilium, built beneath streams in forgetfulness of the flood. After the Trojan War, strife at home drove exiles who returned as Dorians, leading to the founding of Lacedaemon. The Athenian treats this as a fourth settlement to examine, and Megillus eagerly agrees.","ideas":["Earliest government was patriarchal lordship","Legislation began by selecting among family customs","Cities moved down from the hills to the plain over time","The argument returns to laws through the history of Sparta"],"terms":[{"t":"dynasteia (lordship)","d":"Rule by the eldest over a household or clan, the first form of government."},{"t":"Dorians","d":"In the Athenian's account, the returning Achaean exiles who founded the Peloponnesian states."}]},{"i":570,"j":609,"loc":"683b–686b","title":"The Dorian league of three kingdoms","summary":"The Athenian recalls how the Dorian army divided into Argos, Messene and Lacedaemon under Temenus, Cresphontes and Procles and Eurysthenes. Kings and peoples swore to rule and obey under common laws, with the other two cities to aid against any one that rebelled. The lawgivers also had the advantage of distributing land without disputes over property or debts. Yet two kingdoms quickly corrupted their laws, and only Sparta endured, warring with the others. He suggests the league was meant to protect all Hellas against the Assyrian empire, and asks what ruined so promising a confederacy.","ideas":["Mutual oaths bound kings and peoples in three cities","A legislator, like a doctor, cannot only please","Equal land distribution was easy for the Dorians","Only Sparta preserved its original constitution"],"terms":[{"t":"Heraclidae","d":"The sons of Heracles who led the Dorians and divided the army among three kingdoms."},{"t":"Temenus, Cresphontes, Procles and Eurysthenes","d":"The founding kings of Argos, Messene and Lacedaemon."}]},{"i":610,"j":643,"loc":"686b–689a","title":"Foolish prayers and the greatest ignorance","summary":"The Athenian laughs at his own admiration for the Heracleid expedition, observing that people imagine a great possession could achieve wonders if rightly used. All share one desire, that things happen as their soul wishes, yet a wise son would not join a foolish father's prayer, as Theseus cursed Hippolytus. Megillus draws the lesson that one should pray for wisdom, and the Athenian recalls his earlier claim that laws should aim at wisdom, not war. The Dorian kingdoms fell not through cowardice or military ignorance but through the greatest ignorance: hating what one judges good and loving what one judges evil. Those with this ignorance should hold no authority, however clever.","ideas":["The prayer of a fool can bring the opposite of his wish","States and persons should pray for wisdom","The worst ignorance is discord between pleasure and judgment","Authority belongs to those whose souls are in harmony"],"terms":[{"t":"amathia (ignorance)","d":"The worst ignorance: the feeling part of the soul rebelling against reason, like a mob against rulers."},{"t":"symphōnia (harmony)","d":"Agreement of pleasure and reason in the soul, which the Athenian calls the greatest wisdom."}]},{"i":644,"j":680,"loc":"689a–693c","title":"Titles to rule and the moderated Spartan kingship","summary":"The Athenian lists seven claims to rule: parents over children, noble over base, elder over younger, master over slave, stronger over weaker, wise over ignorant, and those chosen by lot. These conflict, and are a source of faction. The kings of Argos and Messene, ignoring Hesiod's saying that the half may be more than the whole, sought power beyond their oaths and destroyed the league. Since no young soul can bear unchecked power, Sparta was saved by a god's gift of two kings, the council of elders, and the ephors, which together kept the mean. Failure to unite the three kingdoms left Hellas weak against Persia, when only Athens and Sparta defended it.","ideas":["There are seven competing titles to authority","Excess of power ruins kings who break their oaths","Sparta's mixed offices moderated royal power","Freedom, wisdom and friendship are one aim of law"],"terms":[{"t":"ephors","d":"Spartan magistrates whose power, the Athenian says, resembled officials chosen by lot and curbed the kings."},{"t":"gerousia (council of elders)","d":"The twenty-eight elders whose power was made equal to that of the kings in important matters."}]},{"i":681,"j":730,"loc":"693d–698a","title":"Persia's decline and the order of honours","summary":"The Athenian names monarchy and democracy as the two mother constitutions, exemplified by Persia and Athens, and says a good state must blend both. Under Cyrus, Persia mixed freedom and rule, with free speech and friendship, but Cyrus left his sons to a luxurious education by women and eunuchs, and Cambyses lost the empire. Darius, not raised as a prince, restored equality and friendship, yet Xerxes was raised like Cambyses, and no truly great king followed. Honours should go first to goods of the soul conditioned by temperance, then to the body, then to wealth. Persia declined through too much despotism, which destroyed friendship.","ideas":["Monarchy and democracy must be mixed in measure","A royal, luxurious education corrupts heirs","Honour soul first, body second, wealth third","Excessive despotism destroys community and loyalty"],"terms":[{"t":"monarchia and dēmokratia","d":"The two mother forms of constitution from which, the Athenian says, the rest derive."},{"t":"sōphrosynē (temperance)","d":"Here the condition that must accompany any virtue that deserves honour."}]},{"i":731,"j":750,"loc":"698a–702d","title":"Athenian freedom, theatrocracy and the new colony","summary":"The Athenian recounts old Athens at the time of the Persian invasions, when a property-based constitution and reverence for the laws, together with fear of Persia, united the citizens at Marathon and Salamis. Under the old laws, music was divided into fixed kinds and audiences listened in silence; then poets mixed genres and claimed pleasure was the only standard, and the theatre audience came to judge. This theatrocracy bred contempt for rulers, parents, laws, oaths and gods. He sums up that a lawgiver seeks freedom, unity and understanding, found in a mean between despotism and liberty. Cleinias reveals he is one of ten charged with founding a Cretan colony, and proposes that they frame its laws together.","ideas":["Reverence for law saved Athens in the Persian Wars","Lawless innovation in music led to general licence","Freedom, unity and wisdom require a mean between extremes","The discussion turns to founding a new Cretan city"],"terms":[{"t":"theatrokratia (theatrocracy)","d":"Rule of the theatre audience, which replaced the judgment of the educated in music."},{"t":"nomoi (laws, songs)","d":"Both laws and a kind of citharoedic song, whose fixed forms the old Athenians kept."}]},{"i":751,"j":776,"loc":"702d–705e","title":"The site of the new city: land, not sea","summary":"Book IV opens with the Athenian asking about the colony's site: Cleinias says it lies eighty stadia inland, with fine harbours, almost self-sufficient, rocky and without neighbours. The Athenian welcomes the distance and the poor soil, since sea trade fills a city with merchants, faithless habits and gold and silver. The lack of ship-timber is also good, for a city should not copy its enemies in what is harmful, and naval warfare teaches hoplites to retreat and honours inferior men. Against the Cretan view that Salamis saved Hellas, he and Megillus credit the land battles of Marathon and Plataea, since the aim is the best life, not mere survival.","ideas":["A coastal, trading city breeds greed and faithless manners","Scarcity of timber keeps the city from becoming a sea power","Naval warfare teaches hoplites to retreat and honours the wrong men","The aim is to live the best life, not just to survive"],"terms":[{"t":"stadion (stade)","d":"A Greek unit of distance of roughly 180 metres; the city lies eighty of them inland."},{"t":"Marathon and Plataea","d":"Land battles against Persia that the Athenian says made the Hellenes better, unlike the sea-fights of Salamis and Artemisium."}]},{"i":777,"j":824,"loc":"705e–711d","title":"Chance, God, art, and the young tyrant","summary":"Cleinias says the colonists will come from all Crete and the Peloponnese, and the Athenian weighs a single-stock colony, united but attached to old laws, against a mixed one, open to new laws but hard to unite. He suggests that chance seems to legislate, yet God governs all, with chance and human art working alongside, as a pilot's skill helps in a storm. Asked what he would pray for, the lawgiver asks for a young, temperate, quick and noble tyrant who meets a great legislator; Cleinias is shocked, but the Athenian argues that change is fastest when rulers lead by example. The real difficulty is finding great power joined with wisdom and temperance, and the section ends with a prayer for God's help in the founding.","ideas":["A colony of one stock is united but resists new laws; a mixed one is the reverse","God, chance and human art all play a part in human affairs","Reform is quickest under one powerful ruler who has the right character","The best laws arise only when supreme power meets wisdom and temperance"],"terms":[{"t":"tyrannos (tyrant)","d":"A sole ruler holding power outside the law; here imagined as young and virtuous so that reform can be swift."},{"t":"sōphrosunē (temperance)","d":"Here the ordinary, inborn self-control that must accompany the other virtues, not the philosophers' sense of prudence."}]},{"i":825,"j":867,"loc":"711d–715d","title":"The age of Cronos and rule of law","summary":"Asked to name their constitutions, Megillus says Sparta resembles tyranny, democracy, aristocracy and monarchy at once, and Cleinias says the same of Cnosus. The Athenian replies that the usual forms are not true constitutions but the rule of one part over the rest, and tells the myth of the age of Cronos, when divine spirits ruled humans as humans rule flocks. People should imitate that life by obeying the immortal element in them, law, which he calls the distribution of mind. He rejects the claim that justice is the interest of the stronger, says laws made for a faction create parties rather than polities, and assigns offices to those most obedient to the laws, since a city whose rulers serve the law is saved.","ideas":["Sparta and Cnosus mix several forms, so they are true polities","Under Cronos, divine spirits ruled humans as humans rule herds","Law is described as the distribution of reason (nous)","Rulers should be servants of the laws; laws serving a faction make a party, not a state"],"terms":[{"t":"Cronos","d":"The god under whose reign, according to the myth, divine spirits ruled humanity in peace and abundance."},{"t":"daimones (spirits)","d":"Beings superior to humans whom Cronos set over human communities as their rulers."},{"t":"nomos (law)","d":"Explained here, through a play on words, as the distribution or ordering of reason (nous)."}]},{"i":868,"j":875,"loc":"715d–716c","title":"The first address to the colonists: God the measure","summary":"The Athenian imagines the colonists arriving and addresses them: God holds the beginning, middle and end of all things, Justice follows him, and the proud man, abandoned by God, is soon destroyed with his house and city. Cleinias agrees that everyone must resolve to follow God. The Athenian adds that God, not man, is the measure of all things, so the friend of God must become like him, temperate and just, and that worship is fitting for the good and wasted by the impure. He sets the order of honours from the Olympian gods down to parents living and dead, whose care repays the greatest debt, and then asks what form the preludes of laws should take.","ideas":["Justice follows God and punishes the proud","God, not man, is the measure; to be dear to him one must become like him","Worship by the good is acceptable, and by the wicked wasted","Honours run from the gods, spirits and heroes to living and dead parents"],"terms":[{"t":"metron (measure)","d":"The standard of all things, which the Athenian says is God, reversing the saying that man is the measure."},{"t":"Nemesis","d":"The messenger of justice who watches over even light words spoken against parents."}]},{"i":876,"j":917,"loc":"716d–724a","title":"Preludes to laws: persuasion joined to force","summary":"The Athenian wants citizens persuaded to virtue, citing Hesiod on the steep road to it, and notes that a poet in his frenzy contradicts himself, while a lawgiver must give one definite rule, such as a defined moderate funeral. He contrasts slave doctors, who give orders without explanation, with free doctors, who talk with their patients and persuade them before treating them. A marriage law is then given in two forms: a bare command with a fine, and a double form explaining that children give people a share in immortality, which Megillus prefers though Sparta uses the short form. The Athenian concludes that laws need preludes, as songs do, that the day's talk has itself been a prelude, and that souls, bodies and property come next.","ideas":["Laws should persuade as well as threaten","The free doctor who explains is better than the slave doctor who just gives orders","Marriage gives humans a share in immortality through their descendants","Every law, or at least the major ones, should have a prelude"],"terms":[{"t":"prooimion (prelude)","d":"A persuasive preface placed before a law to win the citizen's goodwill, like the prelude to a song."},{"t":"the double law","d":"A law stated both as command and penalty and with reasons that persuade, as in the marriage example."}]},{"i":918,"j":936,"loc":"724a–731d","title":"Honouring the soul, body and property","summary":"Book V continues the prelude: after the gods, the soul is the most divine thing a person has, yet people dishonour it by self-flattery, blaming others, indulgence, cowardice, clinging to life, preferring beauty to virtue and taking dishonest gain. The worst penalty for wrongdoing, the Athenian says, is to grow like bad men. The body comes next in honour, but moderate beauty, strength and wealth are safest, and parents should leave their children reverence rather than riches, teaching it by their own example. The address then covers duties to kin, friends, city, strangers and suppliants, whom the gods protect, and praises truthfulness, helping the rulers correct others, striving for virtue without envy, and being both gentle and spirited.","ideas":["The soul is honoured by being made better, not by being flattered","Moderate wealth and bodily goods are safest; leave children reverence, not riches","Wrongs against strangers and suppliants are watched over by the gods","No one is unjust willingly, so the curable deserve pity and the incurable punishment"],"terms":[{"t":"psuchē (soul)","d":"The most divine and most truly personal possession, to be honoured second only to the gods."},{"t":"aidōs (reverence)","d":"A sense of shame and respect, which parents should hand on to children by their own conduct."},{"t":"xenios Zeus","d":"Zeus as protector of strangers, who avenges wrongs done to foreigners and suppliants."}]},{"i":937,"j":942,"loc":"732b–734e","title":"Self-love, and why the virtuous life is pleasanter","summary":"The Athenian names excessive self-love as the greatest evil, because it blinds a person to justice and makes ignorance pass for wisdom, and he urges people to follow their betters and avoid extremes of laughter and grief. Turning to human matters, he argues that the noblest life is also the pleasantest, judged by comparing the amounts and intensities of pleasure and pain. The temperate, wise, brave and healthy lives have gentle feelings in which pleasure exceeds pain, while their opposites have more pain than pleasure, so no one is intemperate willingly. The prelude ends here, and the image of warp and woof introduces the distinction between officials and the laws they administer.","ideas":["Excessive self-love is the root of wrongdoing","Lives are compared by the balance of pleasure over pain","The virtuous life is both nobler and pleasanter","Rulers and ruled differ like warp and woof in weaving"],"terms":[{"t":"philautia (self-love)","d":"The excessive love of oneself that blinds judgment about the just and good."},{"t":"warp and woof","d":"The strong and the softer threads of a fabric, used as an image for officials and the citizens they govern."}]},{"i":943,"j":955,"loc":"735b–741e","title":"Purging the citizens, 5040 lots, the second-best state","summary":"The Athenian says the lawgiver must first purge the citizen body as a herdsman culls his stock, by death or exile at worst and by sending the poor away as a colony at best, though here a good selection is simply assumed. The colony also escapes disputes over redividing land and cancelling debts, and must be founded on freedom from avarice. He fixes the citizens at 5040, a number with fifty-nine divisors, keeps traditional rites intact, and stresses that citizens should know one another. Ranking constitutions, he puts first the state where friends hold everything in common, and calls this city second best: land is divided into lots held as if for the whole city, each passes to one heir, the number of households is fixed, and lots may not be sold.","ideas":["Purging the citizen body comes before legislation","5040 is chosen for how many ways it can be divided","The best state shares everything; this city is the second best","Land lots are inalienable, and the number of households must not change"],"terms":[{"t":"katharmos (purification)","d":"The removal of unsuitable members from the citizen body before a state is founded."},{"t":"klēros (lot)","d":"The family allotment of land and house, held in trust for the city and passed to one heir."},{"t":"koina ta philōn","d":"The saying that friends have all things in common, the mark of the best state."}]},{"i":956,"j":963,"loc":"742c–746d","title":"Money, property classes, and the twelve tribes","summary":"The Athenian bans private gold and silver, allowing only local coin with a state-held Greek currency for missions abroad, and forbids dowries and lending at interest. He argues that no one can be both very rich and very good, since unjust gain doubles income and refusing to spend nobly preserves it; so the very rich are not good and not happy, and soul, body and money rank in that order. Four property classes are set, with the lot as the lower limit and four times the lot as the upper, any excess going to the state. The city stands at the centre, people and land are divided into twelve parts dedicated to gods, and the Athenian defends the plan as a pattern while praising arithmetic and noting how places shape character.","ideas":["No private gold or silver, no dowries and no interest","The very rich cannot also be very good","Four property classes, with wealth capped at four times the lot","The lawgiver should present the ideal pattern, then adapt it where needed"],"terms":[{"t":"timēma (property class)","d":"One of four census ranks used to apportion offices, taxes and distributions."},{"t":"phulē (tribe)","d":"One of twelve divisions of citizens and land, each dedicated to a god."}]},{"i":964,"j":998,"loc":"747b–755a","title":"Choosing the first officials: guardians of the law","summary":"Book VI turns to offices, and the Athenian warns that good laws are useless or harmful in the hands of unsuitable officials, while colonists who are strangers and untrained cannot easily elect well. He proposes that Cnosus help choose the first thirty-seven guardians of the law, nineteen settlers and eighteen Cnosians including Cleinias, and that later all who have served in the army elect them in three rounds from 300 to 100 to 37. Comparing the colony to a child of its mother city, he has a hundred Cnosian elders and a hundred colonists supervise the first appointments. The guardians keep the property registers, prosecute anyone hiding wealth, and must be at least fifty when chosen and retire by seventy.","ideas":["Good laws need suitable officials to work","The mother city helps the colony choose its first officials","Thirty-seven guardians of the law are elected in three rounds","The guardians keep the property registers and punish concealed wealth"],"terms":[{"t":"nomophulakes (guardians of the law)","d":"The thirty-seven senior officials who watch over the laws and the property registers."},{"t":"dokimasia (scrutiny)","d":"The examination of an elected person's fitness before taking office."}]},{"i":999,"j":1002,"loc":"755b–757d","title":"Generals, the council, and two kinds of equality","summary":"The Athenian sets out how generals, brigade commanders and cavalry officers are elected, with counter-nominations and votes by the relevant troops. The council has 360 members, ninety from each property class, chosen over five days, with the upper classes compelled to vote and the final half chosen by lot. He calls this a mean between monarchy and democracy and distinguishes two equalities: one of measure, weight and number, and a higher one, the judgment of Zeus, which gives to each in proportion to virtue and is true justice. The equality of the lot is used sparingly to avoid discontent, and a twelfth of the council keeps watch each month like a crew on a ship.","ideas":["Election combines voting, counter-nomination and the lot","The council of 360 is drawn equally from the four property classes","Proportional equality, according to merit, is true justice","Arithmetical equality by lot is a concession, used as little as possible"],"terms":[{"t":"boulē (council)","d":"The body of 360 councillors, a twelfth of whom are on duty each month."},{"t":"proportional equality","d":"Giving to each in proportion to virtue and education, which the Athenian calls the judgment of Zeus."}]},{"i":1003,"j":1014,"loc":"758a–763c","title":"Priests, interpreters, and the country wardens","summary":"The Athenian provides for temple officials: hereditary priesthoods remain, new priests are chosen by lot so that God decides, must be pure and at least sixty, and serve one year, while interpreters of sacred law are chosen by the tribes and confirmed by Delphi. For the countryside, each tribe supplies five wardens who choose twelve young men aged twenty-five to thirty and rotate through the districts for two years. They build defences, roads, water channels, gymnasia and baths, settle small disputes, and are themselves liable to suits. They eat at common tables, serve themselves without slaves, and count as traitors if they miss duty, since serving well comes before ruling well.","ideas":["Priests are chosen by lot so that the choice is left to God","Sacred law comes from Delphi through appointed interpreters","Young country wardens patrol, fortify and improve the land","One who has not served well will not rule well"],"terms":[{"t":"exēgētai (interpreters)","d":"Officials confirmed by Delphi who explain sacred law and rites."},{"t":"agronomoi (country wardens)","d":"Five officers per district with twelve young men under them, who guard and improve the countryside for two years."}]},{"i":1015,"j":1018,"loc":"763e–766c","title":"City and market wardens, and the education minister","summary":"The Athenian sets up three city wardens from the highest class to oversee roads, buildings and water, and five market wardens from the first two classes, both chosen by vote and then lot and both able to fine offenders; the upper classes must attend the assembly. He then appoints officials for music and gymnastics, some for teaching and some for judging contests, including a director of choruses over forty. Last comes the minister of education, called the greatest office, since a human being well educated becomes the most divine of animals and badly educated the most savage. The minister must be over fifty with legitimate children, is chosen from the guardians by all the magistrates, and serves five years.","ideas":["City and market wardens are chosen by vote followed by lot","Separate officials handle music and gymnastic education and contests","The minister of education holds the most important office","Education decides whether a person becomes divine or savage"],"terms":[{"t":"astunomoi (city wardens)","d":"Three officials in charge of roads, buildings and water supply in the city."},{"t":"agoranomoi (market wardens)","d":"Five officials who keep order in the marketplace and its temples and fountains."}]},{"i":1019,"j":1023,"loc":"766d–768e","title":"The courts of law","summary":"The Athenian says a city without proper courts is no city, and judges must question the parties rather than sit silent. A case goes first to neighbours and arbitrators chosen by the parties, then to a tribal court chosen by lot, and finally to select judges picked yearly, one from every magistracy, who vote openly before the officials who chose them. Public offences concern everyone, so the people share in them, with trials before three senior magistrates, and all citizens should also share in judging private suits so that no one feels excluded from the state. Detailed procedure is postponed to the end, and the account of offices is declared complete.","ideas":["Disputes pass through three levels of court","The highest court is made up of the best person from each magistracy","The people share in judging public wrongs","Taking part in justice gives citizens a sense of belonging to the state"],"terms":[{"t":"dikastēria (courts)","d":"The law courts, treated here as a kind of magistracy."},{"t":"graphē (public suit)","d":"A prosecution for a wrong done to the state, in which all citizens have a stake."}]},{"i":1024,"j":1046,"loc":"769a–772b","title":"Training successors; festivals and the number 5040","summary":"The Athenian compares the lawgiver to a painter who needs successors to preserve and improve his picture, so the aged speakers must make the guardians of the law into lawgivers too. He gives them the single aim of making citizens good, and says exile is better than living under a regime that makes people worse. Returning to 5040, divisible by every number from one to twelve except eleven, he assigns each division a god and twice-monthly festivals for worship and acquaintance, where young men and women dance together modestly so they can choose spouses well. Officials may fill in details over ten years, after which the laws stand unless all officials, the people and the oracles agree to a change.","ideas":["Laws need successors to correct and complete them","The single goal of legislation is to make citizens good","Festivals serve worship and help citizens know one another","After a ten-year trial, the laws change only with unanimous consent"],"terms":[{"t":"5040","d":"The fixed number of households, chosen because it has so many divisors."}]},{"i":1047,"j":1054,"loc":"772d–776b","title":"Marriage: its prelude, fines, and the wedding","summary":"Men are to marry between twenty-five and thirty-five, and the Athenian's prelude urges choosing the marriage that benefits the city rather than oneself, so that rich and poor, hasty and calm natures are mixed like wine tempered with water. Since this cannot be compelled, people are to be charmed into it rather than forced. Men unmarried at thirty-five pay yearly fines to Hera by property class and lose the respect owed to elders, and dowries are strictly limited. He then sets rules on who may betroth a woman, the size and cost of weddings, and sobriety at the time of begetting, since parents imprint themselves on their children, and the couple make their home apart from their parents.","ideas":["Marriages should balance the city's mix of wealth and temperament","The city should be mixed like wine tempered with water","Remaining single past thirty-five brings fines and loss of honour","Begetting children requires sobriety and a sound body"],"terms":[{"t":"proix (dowry)","d":"A marriage portion, sharply limited here so that wives are not insolent and husbands not servile."},{"t":"Hera (Here)","d":"Goddess of marriage, to whom the fines for staying unmarried are paid."}]},{"i":1055,"j":1067,"loc":"776c–778c","title":"Slaves, temples, and the question of walls","summary":"The Athenian takes up slaves, the hardest form of property, citing the disputed Spartan helots and the two views of slaves as loyal or untrustworthy. Revolts such as the Messenian ones lead him to advise against slaves of one nation and one language, and to require that they be treated justly, even more so than equals, since this tests a master's real respect for justice. Slaves are to be given commands, not admonitions or jokes. On buildings, temples are to stand round the agora with courts nearby, and, agreeing with Sparta, he rejects city walls as breeding softness, preferring houses built so that together they form one wall.","ideas":["Slaves are a troublesome form of property","Treating slaves justly shows whether a master truly respects justice","City walls breed softness and false security","If there must be a wall, the uniform houses themselves should form it"],"terms":[{"t":"heilōtes (helots)","d":"The state-owned serf population of Sparta, whose status is called the most disputed form of slavery."}]},{"i":1068,"j":1098,"loc":"778c–783d","title":"Common meals for women; supervising procreation","summary":"The Athenian insists that private life cannot be left unregulated: married men will keep eating at common tables, and Crete and Sparta err in leaving women outside the law, so he proposes common meals for women despite the resistance he expects. Stepping back to the long history of humankind, including human sacrifice and the meatless Orphic life, he concludes that eating, drinking and sexual desire must be governed by fear, law and right reason. He then has women overseers meet daily at the temple of Eileithyia and supervise couples for ten years, with divorce for the childless and loss of honours for offenders. Births are to be registered, marriage ages are set at sixteen to twenty for women and thirty to thirty-five for men, and terms of military service are fixed.","ideas":["Laws must reach into private life as well as public","Women should also have common meals","Food, drink and sexual desire must be governed by fear, law and reason","Women overseers supervise couples during the ten years of child-bearing"],"terms":[{"t":"sussitia (common meals)","d":"Shared public dining, which the Athenian would extend from men to women."},{"t":"Eileithyia","d":"Goddess of childbirth, at whose temple the women overseers of marriage meet."}]},{"i":1099,"j":1163,"loc":"783d–793e","title":"Infant care: motion, fear, and unwritten customs","summary":"Book VII begins education with matters too small for law yet too important to ignore. Bodies grow fastest in infancy, so movement should start before birth and nurses should carry children until three, though the Athenian admits such laws would be mocked. Rocking calms infants as dance cures Corybantic frenzy, because outer motion masters the inner motion of fear, while habitual fear in youth breeds cowardice. Cleinias suggests giving infants many pleasures, but the Athenian argues for the middle state between pleasure and pain, which belongs to God. Such practices are unwritten customs, the bonds of the state that support the written laws like the props of a building.","ideas":["Bodily training starts in the womb and in infancy","Outer motion calms the inner motion of fear","Children should be raised in the mean between pleasure and pain","Unwritten customs hold the written laws together"],"terms":[{"t":"Korubantes (Corybantes)","d":"Ecstatic worshippers whose frenzy is cured by music and dance, given as an example of motion calming the soul."},{"t":"agrapha nomima (unwritten customs)","d":"Ancestral practices that are not laws but bind the state and protect the written code."}]},{"i":1164,"j":1167,"loc":"794a–794d","title":"Early childhood, ambidexterity, dance and wrestling","summary":"Children aged three to six play together at village temples under nurses and twelve matrons, and are punished for self-will without being disgraced. At six the sexes separate, and boys, and girls if willing, learn riding, archery, javelin, sling and heavy arms. The Athenian argues that the difference between right and left hands is not natural but comes from nurses' habits, and points to Scythian archers who use both hands. Gymnastic is divided into dancing and wrestling; showy wrestling tricks are useless, upright wrestling is useful for war, and armed dances like those of the Curetes and of Athena are commended to the young.","ideas":["Children of three to six play under supervision at the village temples","Right- and left-handedness comes from habit and should be corrected","Gymnastic is divided into dancing and wrestling","Armed dances and processions prepare citizens for war and festivals"],"terms":[{"t":"gumnastikē (gymnastic)","d":"Bodily training, divided here into dance and wrestling, both aimed at use in war."},{"t":"Kourētes (Curetes)","d":"Mythical Cretan armed dancers whose dances the city should imitate."}]},{"i":1168,"j":1194,"loc":"795d–799e","title":"Children's games and fixed, consecrated music","summary":"Turning to the Muses, the Athenian makes a bold claim: children's games affect whether laws last, since children who always play the same games leave the serious institutions alone, while those who crave novelty grow up wanting new laws and despising the old. All change except from bad to good is dangerous, in diet as in custom, so reverence for what is old must be built up. Because rhythms and music imitate good and bad characters, he praises the Egyptian practice of consecrating fixed songs and dances to festivals and excluding any others on pain of a charge of impiety. Like a traveller at a crossroads, he pauses over this strange proposal before going on.","ideas":["Novelty in children's games leads to changes in the laws","All change except from bad to good is dangerous","Music imitates character, so its forms should be fixed","Songs and dances are consecrated to the festivals, as in Egypt"],"terms":[{"t":"paidia (play)","d":"Children's games, which shape their later attitude to established laws."}]},{"i":1195,"j":1218,"loc":"799e–802a","title":"Songs as laws: models for poets","summary":"Playing on nomoi, which means both laws and a kind of song, the Athenian declares that songs are laws and that no one may sing or dance against the consecrated models. He offers models for poets: no ill-omened words or wailing at public sacrifices, with dirges left to hired foreign mourners; songs that contain prayers; and care not to pray for evil things such as wealth by mistake. Poets therefore may compose nothing against the city's ideas of the just and good, and must show their work to judges and guardians first. Hymns to gods and heroes and praise of virtuous citizens after death are allowed, and judges over fifty select and revise old songs, preferring severe music, which makes people better.","ideas":["Songs are to be treated as laws","No lamentation or ill-omened words at sacrifices","Poets must submit their work for approval, since they may pray for harmful things","Only the dead may be praised in song; the living are still running their course"],"terms":[{"t":"nomos (law; song-form)","d":"A word meaning both law and a type of traditional song, which the Athenian uses to treat songs as laws."}]},{"i":1219,"j":1238,"loc":"802c–806d","title":"Man as God's plaything; schooling for both sexes","summary":"The Athenian assigns grand, courageous music to men and orderly, temperate music to women, and says that, although human affairs hardly deserve seriousness, God does, and man was made as God's plaything. People wrongly treat war as serious, though it holds no true play or education, and life should rather be spent in sacrifice, song and dance. Megillus objects that this is a low view of mankind, and the Athenian softens it. He then requires public schools with paid foreign teachers, compulsory for all children because they belong to the state, and the same training for girls as for boys, citing the Sauromatian women, since otherwise a state is only half a state, a criticism of Sparta that Megillus resents.","ideas":["Music should suit the different natures of men and women","Man is God's plaything, and this is the best thing about him","Peace and festive play, not war, are the serious business of life","Education is compulsory and the same for girls and boys"],"terms":[{"t":"paignion theou (plaything of God)","d":"The image of human beings as God's toys or puppets, with only a small share of reality."},{"t":"Sauromatides","d":"Women of the Black Sea region who ride and use weapons like men, cited as proof that women can be trained for war."}]},{"i":1239,"j":1257,"loc":"807a–811c","title":"The citizen's day, tutors, letters, and reading","summary":"Citizens with leisure must not live like fattened beasts, the Athenian says, for caring for body and soul is a greater labour than training for the Olympics; they should sleep little and rise early. Boys, the most unruly of animals, need tutors, teachers and correction from any free adult. Addressing the director of education, he sets three years of letters from age ten and three of the lyre from thirteen, no more and no less. He warns that memorising great quantities of poetry and prose is dangerous, and proposes the present conversation, which he calls a kind of poem, as the model text that teachers must learn and approve.","ideas":["Leisure is for the hard work of virtue, not idleness","Short sleep and early rising are fitting for free citizens","Children are to be supervised by tutors, teachers and every free adult","The dialogue itself is proposed as the model for school reading"],"terms":[{"t":"paidagōgos (tutor)","d":"The attendant who supervises a boy once he leaves his mother and nurses."},{"t":"polumathia (much learning)","d":"Wide memorising of poets, which the Athenian calls dangerous for the young."}]},{"i":1258,"j":1288,"loc":"811c–817a","title":"Lyre, military training, dance, comedy and tragedy","summary":"Lyre teaching is to be simple, voice and strings in unison, without the complex harmonies that confuse learners, and paid public teachers will train boys and girls in arms, tactics and riding so that women can defend the city instead of fleeing to the temples. The Athenian divides noble dance into the Pyrrhic, the dance of war, and the Emmeleia, the dance of peace, rejects Bacchic dances as unfit for a city, and fixes all dances once they are consecrated. Comedy must be understood so that one is not ridiculous by mistake, but only slaves and hired foreigners may perform it. To visiting tragic poets the lawgivers reply that their state is itself the truest tragedy, an imitation of the best life, and that outsiders' plays need the magistrates' approval.","ideas":["Lyre teaching should be simple, with notes in unison","Women are trained for war so they can defend the city","There are two noble dances, the Pyrrhic for war and the Emmeleia for peace","The state is itself the noblest tragedy, an imitation of the best life"],"terms":[{"t":"purrichē (Pyrrhic)","d":"The war dance imitating attack and defence."},{"t":"emmeleia","d":"The orderly dance of peace, for people who are moderate in prosperity."},{"t":"kōmōidia (comedy)","d":"Laughable imitation, which citizens should understand but leave to slaves and foreigners to perform."}]},{"i":1289,"j":1348,"loc":"817e–822c","title":"Arithmetic, geometry, incommensurables, and astronomy","summary":"Three studies remain: arithmetic, the measurement of length, area and depth, and the movements of the stars, which all need in part as a divine necessity that not even God opposes, though only a few will study them exactly. The Athenian warns that cleverness with bad upbringing is worse than ignorance and praises Egyptian number games for children. He shames the Greeks for assuming all magnitudes are commensurable and suggests puzzles about incommensurables as a better pastime than draughts. Finally he says it is false to call the sun, moon and planets wanderers, since each moves in one circular path, and correct knowledge of this is needed to avoid blasphemy against the gods.","ideas":["Basic mathematics is a necessary part of knowledge for everyone","Knowledge with bad upbringing is worse than ignorance","Some magnitudes are incommensurable, which most Greeks do not know","The planets each move in one circular path, so calling them wanderers is false"],"terms":[{"t":"asummetron (incommensurable)","d":"A magnitude that cannot be measured exactly by a common unit with another."},{"t":"planētes (planets)","d":"Literally wanderers; the Athenian denies that the heavenly gods truly wander."}]},{"i":1349,"j":1349,"loc":"823b","title":"Praise and blame of hunting","summary":"Before closing education, the Athenian notes that some matters lie between law and admonition, so the best citizen heeds the lawgiver's praise and blame as well as his penalties. He takes hunting as his example, since it covers creatures of sea, air and land, and even human beings. In a prayer to the young he hopes they never take to fishing, piracy, theft, bird-catching or night hunting with nets and traps, and praises only the hunting of land animals with horses, dogs and one's own body. A short law follows on where hunters, fowlers and fishers may go, and this completes the laws on education.","ideas":["Some conduct is guided by praise and blame rather than by penalties","Hunting includes many kinds, including the hunting of men","Only hunting land animals by one's own effort is praised","Netting, fowling, fishing and piracy are discouraged or restricted"],"terms":[{"t":"thēra (hunting)","d":"A wide category of pursuit, used as the example of conduct regulated by praise and blame."}]},{"i":1350,"j":1372,"loc":"823d–831a","title":"Festivals and practising for war in peacetime","summary":"Book VIII fixes the religious calendar, to be confirmed by Delphi: 365 sacrifices so that one is offered every day, twelve monthly feasts for the tribal gods, separate festivals for women, and Pluto, honoured in the twelfth month, as a friend of man. Since no city is safe from wrong unless it is fully good, the Athenian has citizens train for war in peacetime, marching out at least one day a month with women and children, in mock battles with prizes and with praise and blame. Only good men over fifty who have done noble deeds may compose songs for the victors. As boxers spar before a fight, the city's exercises should carry some danger, and accidental deaths count as involuntary homicide, since losing courage would harm the city more.","ideas":["Sacrifices and festivals fill the whole year","Pluto should be honoured, not feared, by fighting men","The city trains for war monthly, including women and children","Realistic war games with some risk test courage"],"terms":[{"t":"Ploutōn (Pluto)","d":"God of the dead, given the twelfth month and called a friend of mankind."}]},{"i":1373,"j":1415,"loc":"831b–834e","title":"Why cities neglect training; military contests","summary":"The Athenian gives two causes why cities neglect war training: love of wealth, which absorbs every citizen and turns the orderly into traders and the bold into robbers, and regimes such as democracy, oligarchy and tyranny, which are really states of faction whose rulers fear strong subjects. Cleinias remarks that he seems especially hostile to love of money. The new city avoids both causes, so only military contests will be held: races in armour over various distances, up to an archer's run of 100 stadia across mountains, with shorter courses for boys and youths and races for girls until marriage. Armed combats replace wrestling, light-armed contests replace the pancration, and horse contests suit Crete, with women joining if they wish.","ideas":["Love of money keeps cities from noble pursuits","Democracy, oligarchy and tyranny are states of faction, not true constitutions","Only contests useful in war are held","Women and girls take part in running and, if they wish, in riding"],"terms":[{"t":"stasiōteiai (states of faction)","d":"The Athenian's name for regimes where rulers coerce unwilling subjects instead of governing them."},{"t":"hoplitēs (hoplite)","d":"A heavy-armed infantryman; races are run in his armour."}]},{"i":1416,"j":1450,"loc":"835a–838e","title":"The problem of desire and three kinds of love","summary":"After leaving the musical contests to the officials, the Athenian raises a harder matter needing a bold speaker guided by reason alone: sexual desire among young people with plenty and leisure, on which Crete and Sparta give no help. Following nature, as before Laius, would condemn same-sex unions, which in his view build neither courage nor temperance. He distinguishes friendship of like with like, of unlike with unlike, and a mixed love torn between bodily desire and love of the beloved's soul, and says the city should want only the love that seeks the beloved's virtue, which Megillus accepts. He then points out that an unwritten law, repeated by everyone and in tragedy, already stops desire within families, and that a lawgiver could consecrate a similar tradition.","ideas":["Leisure and plenty make sexual desire a serious problem for the city","Love may be of the body, of the soul, or a mixture of both","Only love that seeks the beloved's virtue should be welcome","A universal unwritten condemnation is what keeps incest in check"],"terms":[{"t":"erōs (love)","d":"Excessive friendship, divided here into bodily, spiritual and mixed forms."},{"t":"philia (friendship)","d":"Affection between like and like or between unlike, of which love is the intense form."}]},{"i":1451,"j":1477,"loc":"839a–841d","title":"The laws on sexual conduct","summary":"The Athenian proposes a law, hard to establish, confining sex to natural intercourse within marriage for the sake of children, which would bring countless benefits if it were held sacred like the incest taboo. Cleinias agrees that men are more temperate in training, and the Athenian cites athletes such as Iccus of Tarentum who abstained for victory, saying citizens could do the same for the greater victory over pleasure. The first law asks citizens to be no worse than animals that pair faithfully. If that fails, a second standard requires that such acts be rare and hidden, with exposure counted shameful, while piety, love of honour and love of beauty of soul restrain them; relations between men are forbidden, and public offenders lose civic honours, which Megillus accepts.","ideas":["Sex is to be limited to procreation within marriage","Athletes show that abstaining is possible for a goal","Victory over pleasure is the noblest victory","A fallback rule makes concealment honourable and exposure disgraceful"],"terms":[{"t":"Iccus of Tarentum","d":"A famous athlete said to have abstained from sex throughout his training."}]},{"i":1478,"j":1505,"loc":"841e–846d","title":"Farming laws: boundaries, fruit, and water","summary":"Setting aside the details of the common meals, the Athenian turns to food supply: since the city lives from the land alone, it needs far fewer laws, none for merchants, mines or moneylending. The first farming law is that of Zeus of boundaries, forbidding anyone to move a boundary stone, with penalties from the gods and the courts. Encroaching, grazing a neighbour's land, luring away bees, careless burning and close planting are all punished by the country wardens, and water rights follow traditional rules on drawing, digging and drainage. Early picking of grapes and figs is fined, strangers may eat a little on the road, polluted water must be repaid and purified, and minor procedure is left to younger lawmakers.","ideas":["A farming city needs far fewer laws than a trading one","Moving a boundary stone offends Zeus and the law","Water is the most easily polluted element of growth and must be protected","Small procedural details are left to younger legislators"],"terms":[{"t":"Zeus Horios","d":"Zeus as guardian of boundaries, whose law is the first of the farming laws."},{"t":"Arcturus","d":"The star whose rising marks the vintage season, before which autumn fruit may not be picked."}]},{"i":1506,"j":1522,"loc":"846e–849c","title":"Craftsmen, trade, distribution of food, and metics","summary":"No citizen or citizen's servant may practise a craft, since keeping the city's order is a full-time art, and each craftsman may practise only one. There are no customs duties, unneeded luxuries are not imported, needed goods are not exported, and there is no retail trade for profit. Following Cretan custom, produce is divided into twelve parts and then into three equal shares, for citizens, slaves, and craftsmen and foreigners, of which only the third is sold. Twelve hamlets with temples and markets are laid out, craftsmen are placed among the villages, and the market wardens set monthly sale days, ban credit and record changes in property. Metics may settle for twenty years, untaxed, if they practise a craft, and may stay longer only with permission.","ideas":["Citizens do not practise crafts, and each craftsman has one craft only","There is no retail trade for profit and no customs duties","Produce is shared equally among citizens, slaves and foreigners","Resident foreigners may stay twenty years, untaxed"],"terms":[{"t":"metoikoi (metics)","d":"Resident foreigners who practise crafts and may stay twenty years."},{"t":"kapēleia (retail trade)","d":"Small-scale trading for profit, barred to citizens and left to foreigners."}]},{"i":1523,"j":1551,"loc":"849d–857a","title":"Temple robbery, treason, and the conduct of trials","summary":"Book IX turns to criminal suits. The Athenian admits it is shameful to legislate for crimes in a well-ordered city, but since he is a man legislating for men, some citizen may prove incurably hard. He gives a prelude to the would-be temple robber, calling the urge an inherited madness to be met with expiations, good company, or death. The law then punishes slaves and strangers by branding, flogging and expulsion, and citizens with death, without punishing their children or confiscating lots. He fixes limits on fines, an open three-day trial procedure before guardians and chosen magistrates, and death for anyone who subverts the constitution by violence.","ideas":["Laws are needed because lawgivers now legislate for men, not sons of gods","Punishment aims to make the offender better or less bad","Children are not punished for a father's crime, and the lots stay intact"],"terms":[{"t":"prooimion (prelude)","d":"The persuasive address that precedes the law and tries to win obedience before threatening."},{"t":"hierosulia (temple robbery)","d":"Robbery of sacred things, treated with treason as a crime of the incurable."}]},{"i":1552,"j":1575,"loc":"857b–859b","title":"Laws as teachers: the legislator's writings","summary":"After the Athenian proposes a single penalty, double restitution, for every theft, Cleinias objects that thefts differ greatly and deserve different penalties. The Athenian concedes the point and recalls his image of the slave doctor who would mock the free doctor for educating his patient rather than curing him. Cleinias notes that, unlike practical lawgivers, they are at leisure to gather materials like builders collecting stones. The Athenian argues that a legislator's writings should be the noblest in the city, teaching what is good and just, and that laws should speak like loving, wise parents rather than tyrants posting decrees.","ideas":["Theft is first given one penalty, double restitution, until Cleinias objects","Discussing laws educates citizens and does not merely command them","Legal writings should be the standard for all other writings"],"terms":[{"t":"nomothetēs (legislator)","d":"The lawgiver, here cast as a teacher of the good and just rather than a mere commander."}]},{"i":1576,"j":1623,"loc":"859c–862d","title":"Is all injustice involuntary? Hurt versus injustice","summary":"The Athenian notes a confusion in common speech: just punishments are called both just and shameful, so the just and the honourable come apart. He then restates his view that no one is unjust willingly, and faces the problem that all legal systems distinguish voluntary from involuntary wrongs. His answer is to separate harm from injustice: accidental harm is not injustice at all, and a benefit wrongly given may be unjust. Harm calls for compensation and reconciliation; injustice is a disease of the soul to be cured by every means, and only the incurable should be put to death.","ideas":["The many speak inconsistently of just but shameful punishments","No one does injustice willingly, yet the law must still grade offences","Harm is repaired by compensation; injustice is cured as a disease","Death is reserved for the incurable, as a benefit to others"],"terms":[{"t":"blabē (hurt, harm)","d":"Damage done to another, which the law repairs whether or not it was intended."},{"t":"adikia (injustice)","d":"A disorder of the soul in which passions rule, distinct from the harm it may cause."}]},{"i":1624,"j":1652,"loc":"862e–865d","title":"The causes of wrongdoing and defining injustice","summary":"At Cleinias' request the Athenian names the causes of wrongdoing: passion or anger, which overthrows by force; pleasure, which works by persuasion and deceit; and ignorance, either simple or double, the latter joined with a conceit of wisdom. He defines injustice as the tyranny in the soul of anger, fear, pleasure, pain, envy and desire, whether or not harm results. Action ruled by the opinion of the best is just, even if it sometimes errs. He adds that deeds done openly by violence differ from those done secretly by deceit, which deserve harsher laws. Offenders who acted in madness, disease, extreme age or childishness pay only for the damage, with a year's exile for bloodshed.","ideas":["Three sources of wrongdoing: passion, pleasure, and ignorance","Double ignorance, the conceit of knowledge, breeds great crimes when powerful","Justice is the rule of the best opinion in the soul, even when mistaken"],"terms":[{"t":"thumos (passion, anger)","d":"The forceful, irrational element of the soul that drives many offences."},{"t":"double ignorance","d":"Ignorance joined to the belief that one knows, the source of the worst errors."}]},{"i":1653,"j":1658,"loc":"865e–866e","title":"Involuntary homicide and its purifications","summary":"The Athenian begins the laws of homicide with killings that are violent but involuntary. Deaths in games, war or military exercises, and of patients under physicians, are free of guilt after purification. Other accidental killers must purify themselves, compensate a slave's owner, and withdraw from the victim's haunts for a year. He cites an old tale that the newly dead victim is angry and disturbs his killer. A kinsman who fails to prosecute a disobedient killer takes the pollution on himself, and a stranger who kills unintentionally is banished.","ideas":["Accidental killing in games, war or medicine is guiltless after purification","Year-long exile keeps the killer from the haunts of the newly dead","Kin of the victim must prosecute, or the pollution falls on them"],"terms":[{"t":"miasma (pollution)","d":"The religious stain of bloodshed, which must be cleansed by purification and exile."},{"t":"exēgētai (interpreters)","d":"Religious officials who declare the proper purifications in cases of blood."}]},{"i":1659,"j":1681,"loc":"867a–870e","title":"Homicide in anger, within the family and in self-defence","summary":"The Athenian treats killings done in passion as lying between voluntary and involuntary, only images of each. Sudden killing without premeditation brings two years' exile; killing after nursing one's anger brings three. On their return, twelve judges assess the exiles, and a repeat offender is banished for good. He then sets penalties for killings of and by slaves, parents, children, spouses and siblings, including permanent separation of the slayer from the family's rites and table. A child who kills a parent in anger is put to death unless the dying parent forgives him. Killing in self-defence is free of guilt, except a slave who kills a freeman.","ideas":["Killings in anger resemble the voluntary or involuntary, but are neither","Premeditated anger is punished more than sudden anger","Parricide in anger is punished by death, unless forgiven by the parent"],"terms":[{"t":"pro-bouleusis (premeditation)","d":"Planning a deed beforehand, which makes a killing in anger resemble a voluntary one."}]},{"i":1682,"j":1702,"loc":"871a–875a","title":"Wilful murder, kin-killing, suicide and justified killing","summary":"The Athenian names the causes of wilful murder: greed for wealth, which he says the many wrongly rank first among goods, then ambition and jealousy, then cowardly fear of exposure. As a prelude he cites the teaching of the mysteries that murderers are punished below and suffer the same deed in another life. The law outlaws the murderer from public places, requires kin to prosecute, and punishes conviction with death and burial outside the victim's land. Murder of kin is punished by death at a crossroads, stoning of the body by magistrates, and casting it out unburied. Suicides from cowardice are buried alone and unmarked, animals and objects that kill are tried and cast out, and killing a night thief or a violator is guiltless.","ideas":["Wealth ranks third, after soul and body, and greed is the chief cause of murder","The mysteries teach that murder is repaid in kind in a later life","Kin-murder pollutes the city and is expiated publicly"],"terms":[{"t":"pleonexia (greed)","d":"Insatiable desire for gain, named as the main source of wilful murder."}]},{"i":1703,"j":1716,"loc":"875b–877b","title":"Why law is needed, and what judges may decide","summary":"Turning to wounds, the Athenian offers a general prelude on law. Without laws men would live like savage beasts, because no one easily sees that the art of politics serves the common good before the private. Even someone who knew this would be corrupted by unchecked power. A mind that was true and free would need no law, since no law is above knowledge, but such a mind hardly exists, so law and order are the second best. He then argues that some matters must be left to the courts and others fixed by the legislator, and that good, well-tested judges like theirs may be trusted to set many penalties.","ideas":["The true political art puts the common good before the private","Absolute power corrupts even someone who knows the good","Law is second best, below free and true intelligence","Good courts may be left to set penalties within a given outline"],"terms":[{"t":"nous (mind)","d":"Intelligence that, if truly free and in accord with nature, would stand above all law."}]},{"i":1717,"j":1728,"loc":"877c–879d","title":"Laws on wounding","summary":"The Athenian treats wounding with intent to kill as murder. Because chance spared the victim, the penalty is lifelong exile to a neighbouring city rather than death. Children who intentionally wound parents, slaves who wound masters, and siblings who wound siblings are put to death; spouses are exiled for life. When a house is left without heir through crime, it is purified and a son from a virtuous, fortunate family is installed, since every house belongs to the state. Wounding in anger is paid at double or fourfold damages, with military service owed in place of a disabled man. Accidental wounding is paid simply as damage.","ideas":["Attempted murder is judged as murder but punished with exile","The 5040 houses belong to the state, which must keep them filled","Passionate wounding lies between voluntary and involuntary, like homicide"],"terms":[{"t":"klēros (lot)","d":"The fixed allotment of land of each of the 5040 households, kept intact through heirs."}]},{"i":1729,"j":1738,"loc":"879e–881d","title":"Assault and reverence for elders and parents","summary":"The Athenian bases the law of assault on the honour owed to elders. A citizen must treat anyone twenty years older as a father or mother and must never strike a stranger, but take him to the wardens. Equals in age may defend themselves with bare hands, and fighting after forty is disgraceful. Bystanders must separate those who strike an elder, under fines graded by property class. Since stories of punishment below do not deter men who strike their parents, the law imposes harsh penalties: exile from the city, exclusion from temples, death on return, and pollution for anyone who associates with the offender. Rules for slaves who strike freemen end Book IX.","ideas":["The young must reverence those old enough to be their parents","Bystanders are bound by law to help a person attacked","Striking a parent merits penalties as close as possible to those below"],"terms":[]},{"i":1739,"j":1766,"loc":"881e–887a","title":"Impiety: the three wrong beliefs about the gods","summary":"Book X opens with a general law against violence and seizure of others' goods, the worst cases being insults to sacred things. The Athenian says no one who believes in the gods commits impiety unless he thinks they do not exist, or do not care for men, or can be bought off with sacrifices. He imagines such men asking to be persuaded before they are threatened. Cleinias thinks the sun, stars, seasons and universal belief prove the gods, but the Athenian replies that the impious are moved by an ignorance that passes for wisdom. He blames the old theogonies and, above all, newer thinkers who call the heavenly bodies mere earth and stones.","ideas":["Three impious beliefs: the gods do not exist, do not care, or can be bribed","The impious ask to be persuaded with argument rather than threatened","The danger lies in an ignorance that takes itself for wisdom"],"terms":[{"t":"asebeia (impiety)","d":"Offence against the gods in word or deed, traced here to false beliefs about them."}]},{"i":1767,"j":1805,"loc":"887b–891b","title":"The young atheist and the doctrine of nature, art and chance","summary":"Cleinias insists that proving the gods' existence and goodness is the best prelude to all their laws, however long it runs. The Athenian addresses a young unbeliever gently, noting that no one who denied the gods in youth kept that view into old age. He then sets out the doctrine of certain wise men: the greatest things come from nature and chance, lesser ones from art. On this view the elements formed the heavens without mind, the gods exist only by convention, and justice is mere agreement, so the young are taught that might is right. The three agree that the lawgiver should persuade as well as threaten, and that written laws can be studied again and again.","ideas":["Denial of the gods rarely lasts from youth to old age","The opponents rank nature and chance before art and mind","If justice is only convention, the life according to nature becomes domination"],"terms":[{"t":"phusis (nature)","d":"For the opponents, the blind elemental order prior to mind, art and law."},{"t":"technē (art)","d":"Human craft, which the opponents treat as late, mortal and derived from nature."}]},{"i":1806,"j":1885,"loc":"891c–896d","title":"Soul as the first motion, prior to body","summary":"The Athenian finds the root error in thinkers who make fire, water, earth and air first and soul later. He argues that soul is older than body, so mind, art and law are prior to hard and soft, and soul exists by nature in the truest sense. Offering to cross the river of argument first himself, he lists ten kinds of motion, ending with motion that moves others but not itself and motion that moves both itself and others. Self-motion must be first, since a chain of things moved by others needs a beginning, and it would arise first if all things were at rest. Self-motion is life, and its definition is the definition of soul, so soul is the first cause of all change.","ideas":["Soul is older than body and is the source of all motion","Ten kinds of motion, crowned by self-motion","The name 'soul' and the definition 'self-moving motion' denote the same thing"],"terms":[{"t":"psuchē (soul)","d":"Defined here as the motion able to move itself, and so the origin of all change."},{"t":"autokinēsis (self-motion)","d":"Motion that begins from itself, which must come first among all motions."}]},{"i":1886,"j":1941,"loc":"896e–900a","title":"The best soul guides the heavens; all is full of gods","summary":"From the priority of soul the Athenian infers that character, wish and reasoning come before bodily qualities, and that soul causes good and evil alike. There must be at least two souls, one beneficent and one the opposite. Since the heavens move in a regular circle, like the revolution of mind, the best soul must guide them. He reasons that a soul moves the sun, whether from inside it, through an external body, or by some wondrous power, and that this soul is a god. The same holds for the moon, stars and seasons, so all things are full of gods. The atheist must either refute the argument or believe.","ideas":["At least two souls: one doing good and one the contrary","Mind's motion resembles a sphere turning in one place","Each heavenly body is moved by a soul which is a god"],"terms":[{"t":"periphora (revolution)","d":"Uniform circular motion in one place, the image of the movement of mind."}]},{"i":1942,"j":2006,"loc":"900b–903c","title":"The gods care for small things as well as great","summary":"The Athenian turns to the man who believes in gods but thinks they neglect human affairs, misled by the prosperity of the wicked. He argues that the gods are good, and that sloth and carelessness are vices, so the gods cannot neglect anything from laziness. Neither can they neglect from ignorance or weakness, since they see and know all and have all power. Mortal creatures are the gods' possessions, and the best owners do not neglect their property. Like physicians, pilots and builders, a good craftsman tends small parts as well as large, so it would be impious to make God inferior to human workmen.","ideas":["The prosperity of the wicked is what tempts men to think the gods careless","Neglect could only come from sloth or ignorance, and neither fits the gods","Small things are easier to care for than great"],"terms":[]},{"i":2007,"j":2024,"loc":"903d–905d","title":"Consolation: the part exists for the whole","summary":"Having refuted the young man, the Athenian offers him consolation. The ruler of the universe orders everything for the good of the whole, and each person is a small part made for the whole, not the whole for him. Soul and body, once born, are indestructible, and the king arranged a place for each kind of soul while leaving the shaping of character to each one's will. Souls move according to their changes: the worse sink toward Hades, while the better, joined with divine virtue, pass to a holier place. No one, however small or high, escapes this justice of the gods, which also explains the apparent good fortune of the wicked.","ideas":["Each part is made for the whole, not the whole for the part","The gods place souls; individuals shape their own character","Like goes to like across lives, and none escapes divine justice"],"terms":[{"t":"dikē (justice of the gods)","d":"The order by which better souls go to better places and worse to worse."}]},{"i":2025,"j":2054,"loc":"905e–908b","title":"The gods cannot be bribed","summary":"The Athenian addresses the third opponent, who thinks the gods can be appeased by gifts. If gods are rulers of the heavens, they resemble guardians such as pilots, charioteers, generals, physicians, farmers or shepherds. He describes an unending battle between good and evil in which gods and spirits are our allies and we are their possessions. To think the gods pardon wrongdoers for a share of the spoils is to compare them to dogs bought off by wolves, or pilots and charioteers who are bribed. Cleinias agrees that this is the worst impiety. The Athenian says he spoke with heat because he is zealous against evil men.","ideas":["Bribable gods would be worse guardians than ordinary dogs or men","Life is an undying struggle in which the gods fight on our side","The three theses are now taken as shown: gods exist, care, and cannot be bribed"],"terms":[]},{"i":2055,"j":2063,"loc":"908c–910a","title":"Laws against impiety; three prisons; no private shrines","summary":"The law requires anyone who sees impiety to report it to the magistrates. The Athenian sets up three prisons: a common one near the agora, a House of Reformation near the nocturnal council, and one in a wild central region for retribution. He distinguishes six kinds of impious people. Unbelievers of just character, who err from folly, spend at least five years in the House of Reformation conversing only with council members, and die if they relapse. The crafty kind, who pose as diviners and claim to charm gods and the dead for money, are imprisoned for life and cast out unburied. Private shrines and rites are forbidden, and grave impiety in sacrifice is punished by death.","ideas":["Unbelief joined to just character differs from unbelief joined to cunning","Foolish unbelievers are to be reformed; the crafty are imprisoned for life","All worship must take place in public temples"],"terms":[{"t":"sōphronistērion (House of Reformation)","d":"The prison where misguided unbelievers are held and counselled by the nocturnal council."}]},{"i":2064,"j":2088,"loc":"910b–917b","title":"Treasure-trove, lost property, slaves and sales","summary":"Book XI regulates dealings between citizens under a simple principle: do not touch what is another's without consent. The Athenian forbids lifting buried treasure not laid down by oneself or one's ancestors, since justice in the soul is worth more than wealth, and he refers such cases to Delphi. Left property belongs to the goddess of ways and must not be taken. He then gives rules for disputed ownership, recovery of slaves, and the duties and limits of freedmen, who may not grow richer than their former masters. Sales must be made for cash in the agora, and buyers may return slaves found to have hidden diseases, or slaves who had committed homicide.","ideas":["Do not take up what you did not lay down","Justice in the soul is better than gain from treasure","Freedmen and wealthy foreigners may not remain beyond fixed limits"],"terms":[{"t":"metic (resident alien)","d":"A free foreigner living in the city, allowed to stay only under conditions."}]},{"i":2089,"j":2113,"loc":"917c–922a","title":"Adulteration, retail trade, contracts and craftsmen","summary":"The Athenian treats adulteration as falsehood and forbids calling the gods to witness any deceit. Sellers in the agora may ask only one price and may not praise their wares or swear about them, and fraud is punished by flogging. He explains that retail trade is useful by nature, since it equalizes goods through money. It is despised because most people's desires are unbounded, as when innkeepers hold travellers to ransom. No citizen may be a retail trader; trade is left to metics and strangers, with profits fixed by the guardians. Contracts must be kept, craftsmen are dedicated to Hephaestus, Athena and Ares and must not cheat or go unpaid, and the city must honour its soldiers.","ideas":["Adulteration is a lie and a false oath before the gods","Retail trade is good in principle but corrupts through unlimited desire","Wealth and poverty are the two enemies of the soul"],"terms":[{"t":"kapēleia (retail trade)","d":"Petty trade and inn-keeping, barred to citizens and left to foreigners."}]},{"i":2114,"j":2151,"loc":"922b–927c","title":"Wills, intestacy and the marriage of heiresses","summary":"The Athenian notes that a dying man wants full control of his property, and that earlier legislators granted it too easily. In a prelude he tells the testator that he and his possessions belong to the family and the state, and that the law will judge for the whole. The law then fixes how the lot passes to one son, how other sons, daughters and adopted children are provided for, and how guardians of orphans are named. When a man dies intestate leaving daughters, they must marry their nearest kinsmen in a set order, to keep the lot in the family. Since such rules may be hard in particular cases, disputes go to fifteen guardians of the law as arbiters.","ideas":["A man's goods belong to his family and the state, not to him alone","The lot must pass intact to a single heir","Kin-marriage rules give way to arbiters where they cause real hardship"],"terms":[{"t":"epiklēros (heiress)","d":"A daughter through whom an estate passes, married to a kinsman to keep the lot in the family."}]},{"i":2152,"j":2168,"loc":"927d–930e","title":"Orphans, disowning sons, insanity, divorce and remarriage","summary":"The Athenian calls orphanhood a second birth and makes the guardians of the law fathers to orphans. He appeals to the belief that the souls of the dead care for their children, and that the aged see and avenge wrongs. Guardians must treat an orphan's goods as their own or better, and wrongs to orphans are punished double. A father may disown a son only with the vote of most of his kin, and the disowned son may be adopted or sent to a colony. A son may have an insane father declared incompetent with the counsel of the eldest guardians. Officials try to reconcile ill-matched spouses or find them new partners, and further rules settle remarriage and the status of children of slaves.","ideas":["The dead and the aged watch over the treatment of orphans","No house may be added to or lost from the 5040","Family breakdowns are handled by kin councils and officials"],"terms":[]},{"i":2169,"j":2186,"loc":"931a–933b","title":"Parents as living images of the gods","summary":"The Athenian prefaces the law on parents with a point about worship: we honour some gods directly and others through lifeless statues. Aged parents and grandparents at the hearth are images more potent than any statue. Myths of Oedipus, Amyntor and Theseus show that the gods hear parents' curses, so they must also hear their blessings. Good men are glad when parents live long, while bad men fear them. The law lets neglected parents complain to the guardians and the women supervising marriages. Younger offenders are flogged or bound, and older ones are tried by the eldest citizens.","ideas":["An honoured parent is a better image of the divine than a statue","Parents' prayers and curses are heard by the gods","Neglect of parents is punished, with informers protected"],"terms":[{"t":"agalma (image, statue)","d":"A sacred image of a god, compared here to living aged parents."}]},{"i":2187,"j":2201,"loc":"933c–936b","title":"Poisoning and sorcery, and the aims of penalty","summary":"The Athenian separates two kinds of poisoning: drugs acting on bodies by nature, and sorcery, spells and wax images that frighten the credulous. Such things are hard to know about, so people should be warned against using them rather than argued out of their fears. A physician who harms with drugs, or a prophet or diviner who harms by sorcery, is put to death; others are punished as the court decides. For theft and violence, damages must make good the loss, and a further penalty is added to correct the offender. Penalty does not undo the past; it aims to make the offender and onlookers hate injustice. The legislator sketches cases like a painter and leaves details to the judge.","ideas":["Harm by drugs differs from supposed harm by magic","Punishment looks to the future, not to undoing what is done","The judge works with the legislator, who aims like an archer"],"terms":[{"t":"pharmakeia (poisoning, sorcery)","d":"Harm by drugs or by spells, treated in one law with two parts."}]},{"i":2202,"j":2210,"loc":"936c–938a","title":"Madmen, abuse and comedy, beggars, witnesses and advocates","summary":"Families must keep madmen at home, and no one may abuse another, since words light as air breed great hatreds and feed the angry part of the soul. Ridicule spoken in anger is banned. Comic and iambic poets may not mock any citizen, and joking verse is allowed only with the education supervisor's licence. There must be no beggars, since a virtuous person in a well-ordered city will not be left destitute. The Athenian then regulates damage done by slaves and animals, the duties of witnesses, and charges of false witness, and three convictions bar a man from giving evidence. Finally he condemns paid advocacy that wins unjust causes: those who practise it for gain are put to death.","ideas":["Abuse and ridicule spoken in anger corrupt the soul","Comic mockery of citizens is forbidden","Rhetoric for hire perverts justice and is punished severely"],"terms":[{"t":"kakēgoria (abuse)","d":"Evil-speaking against another, banned in public places and controlled by elders."}]},{"i":2211,"j":2237,"loc":"938b–945e","title":"Envoys, public theft, military discipline and lost arms","summary":"Book XII begins with penalties for heralds and envoys who carry false messages. The Athenian denies the poets' tales that gods steal or rob, and rules that a citizen who robs the public is incurable and must die. In war no one should act without a commander, and from youth citizens should practise ruling and being ruled together, since anarchy has no place. He prescribes trials by fellow soldiers for failing to serve or deserting, and prizes of valour. He then distinguishes losing one's arms by force from throwing away the shield in cowardice, citing Patroclus. Since a coward cannot be turned into a woman as Caeneus was turned into a man, he is barred from service and fined.","ideas":["Public theft by an educated citizen marks him as incurable","Soldiers must never act on their own, even in small things","Throwing away a shield in cowardice differs from losing it by misfortune"],"terms":[{"t":"rhipsaspia (throwing away the shield)","d":"Abandoning one's arms in cowardice, distinguished from losing them by force."}]},{"i":2238,"j":2262,"loc":"946a–950b","title":"The examiners, their honours, and suits without oaths","summary":"The Athenian calls the examiners of magistrates crucial to whether a state holds together or splits into many. Each year the citizens choose three men over fifty, in a narrowing vote held in the precinct of Helios and Apollo. These become examiners, who audit every magistracy and post their judgments in the agora, with appeal to the select judges. Examiners are honoured as priests of Apollo and Helios, and their funerals have white robes, hymns, and a vaulted tomb with yearly games. Rhadamanthus decided suits by oath because men then believed in the gods, but since belief has changed, litigants may no longer swear, while judges and electors still take oaths.","ideas":["Examiners must excel the magistrates they review","Corrupt scrutiny dissolves the bonds of a constitution","Party oaths are dropped because belief in the gods has weakened"],"terms":[{"t":"euthunoi (examiners)","d":"Officials who audit magistrates at the end of their terms."}]},{"i":2263,"j":2285,"loc":"950c–954a","title":"Foreign travel, observers abroad, and receiving strangers","summary":"The Athenian says contact with foreigners mixes customs, but expelling strangers would look savage, and a good reputation matters. No one under forty may travel abroad except on public business. Delegations go to the great festivals, and observers aged fifty to sixty may travel to seek out inspired men who exist even in badly ordered cities. On return, an observer reports to the council that reviews the laws, made up of priests, the ten eldest guardians, the education supervisor and younger companions, and meeting at dawn. A corrupted observer is silenced or put to death. Four kinds of visitors, from merchants to distinguished observers, are received in set ways under Zeus of strangers.","ideas":["A city needs contact with others to understand its laws, not just follow habit","Wise men can arise in badly governed cities and should be sought out","Visitors are received with hospitality but kept from introducing novelties"],"terms":[{"t":"theōros (observer)","d":"A citizen sent abroad to study other institutions and report to the council."},{"t":"xenēlasia (expulsion of strangers)","d":"Driving out foreigners, a practice the Athenian rejects as harsh."}]},{"i":2286,"j":2302,"loc":"954b–956e","title":"Sureties, searches, prescription and offerings","summary":"The Athenian sets procedural rules: sureties must be in writing before witnesses, and a house may be searched by someone who swears and enters stripped of clothing. Claims to goods openly used lapse after fixed periods. Preventing a party or witness from attending a trial voids the suit, and preventing a competitor brings damages. Receiving stolen goods is punished like theft, and harbouring an exile, private war or peace, or taking gifts for public service is punished by death. Property is valued for taxation. Offerings to the gods should be moderate: no gold, silver, ivory, bronze or iron, but simple wood, stone, a month's weaving, or a painting made in a day.","ideas":["Possession openly held for a set time cannot be challenged","Public servants must serve without gifts","The gods want modest offerings, not costly ones"],"terms":[]},{"i":2303,"j":2323,"loc":"957a–960c","title":"Courts, enforcement of judgments, and burial","summary":"The Athenian sets out three tiers of courts: arbiters chosen by both parties, village and tribe courts, and the select judges, with rising penalties for whoever loses on appeal. He says the study of good laws improves the learner most, since nomos is akin to nous. Judges should keep the legislator's writings as an antidote to all other words. Judgments are enforced by handing over the loser's property, and defiance of a court is punished by death. For burial, tombs must be on land unfit for farming, with modest mounds and costs set by class. The legislator teaches that the soul is the true self and the corpse only an image.","ideas":["Appeals are allowed but cost more each time","Law (nomos) is named after mind (nous)","The soul is the real person; the body is a shade"],"terms":[{"t":"nomos (law)","d":"Law, linked by wordplay to nous, mind, as its rational distribution."}]},{"i":2324,"j":2363,"loc":"960d–963a","title":"Preserving the laws: the council as anchor of the state","summary":"The Athenian says the work is complete only when the laws are made to last, like the threads that Atropos, the third Fate, makes unchangeable. He recalls the council of the ten eldest guardians, the prize-winners for virtue, returned observers, and young men they co-opt, all meeting at dawn. This council will be the city's anchor. Like the soul and head in an animal, or pilot and crew in a ship, a city needs a saving power that knows its aim, just as a general knows victory and a physician health. A state lacking such an institution acts by chance, and Cleinias recognizes the nocturnal council as that power.","ideas":["Laws need a power that preserves them once made","Every art saves its object by knowing its single aim","The council must know the aim of the city"],"terms":[{"t":"nukterinos sullogos (nocturnal council)","d":"The council of elders and chosen younger men meeting before dawn to preserve the laws."}]},{"i":2364,"j":2419,"loc":"963b–966d","title":"The unity of virtue and the guardians' knowledge","summary":"The Athenian explains that states go astray because their lawgivers aim at different things: power, wealth or freedom. He recalls that their laws aim at virtue, which has four kinds led by wisdom. Yet no one has said what the political mind looks to. The guardians must know how courage, which even beasts and children have, differs from wisdom, and also what makes the four one virtue. He compares the city to a body, with the younger guardians as eyes and the elders as mind. Guardians must see the one form in many things, know how the good and the noble are one, explain this in words, and labour over every proof about the gods.","ideas":["States err because they have many aims rather than one","Guardians must know why four virtues are called one","The best method is to see one idea across many things","Guardians, unlike ordinary citizens, must know the proofs about the gods"],"terms":[{"t":"aretē (virtue)","d":"Excellence, one in name and nature though divided into courage, temperance, justice and wisdom."},{"t":"idea (form)","d":"The single character gathered from many cases, which guardians must grasp."}]},{"i":2420,"j":2446,"loc":"966e–969d","title":"Two grounds of belief and the city handed to the council","summary":"The Athenian names the two grounds of belief in the gods: that soul is the eldest and most divine of things, and that the stars move in order under mind. Astronomy leads to atheism only for those who think soul younger than body and see the heavens as stones and earth. A true ruler must grasp these principles, together with music and the related studies, and connect them with the laws. He proposes that the nocturnal council be the legal guard of the state, but says its curriculum cannot be written in advance. He offers to help, and if the council is established the city can be handed over to it. Megillus and Cleinias resolve to keep the Stranger as a partner in the founding.","ideas":["Soul's priority and the order of the stars lead to piety","Only one who grasps these may rule; others should obey","The council's studies cannot be fixed in writing beforehand"],"terms":[{"t":"astronomia (astronomy)","d":"Study of the heavens, which properly understood shows mind ordering the universe."}]}]});